","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = ΔS°(products) - ΔS°(reactants)

where ΔS° is the standard entropy change for each substance.

The reaction is:

3CO(g) + O3(g) → 3CO2(g)

We need the standard entropy values (ΔS°) for CO, CO2, and O3 at 298 K. These values can be found in thermodynamic tables or thermodynamic tables in the literature.

From standard thermodynamic tables, we have:

ΔS°(CO) = 195.1 J/mol·K
ΔS°(CO2) = 238.1 J/mol·K
ΔS°(O3) = 201.6 J/mol·K

Now, we can calculate the change in entropy:

ΔS = 3 * ΔS°(CO2) - (3 * ΔS°(CO) + ΔS°(O3))

ΔS = 3 * 238.1 J/mol·K - (3 * 195.1 J/mol·K + 201.6 J/mol·K)

ΔS = 714.3 J/mol·K - (585.3 J/mol·K + 201.6 J/mol·K)

ΔS = 714.3 J/mol·K - 786.9 J/mol·K

ΔS = -72.6 J/mol·K

The change in entropy for the reaction is -72.6 J/mol·K.",relevance,"# Task Description
