The coefficients are multiplied by the number of atoms in each molecule to find the total number of atoms of each element on both sides of the equation. For example, the left side of the equation has 4 hydrogen atoms (2 H atoms per H₂ molecule) and 2 oxygen atoms (2 O atoms per O₂ molecule). The right side of the equation has 4 hydrogen atoms (2 H atoms per H₂O molecule) and 2 oxygen atoms (1 O atom per H₂O molecule). Since the number of atoms of each element is the same on both sides of the equation, the equation is balanced.

Balancing chemical equations involves balancing the number of atoms of each element on both sides of the equation. This can be done by multiplying the coefficients of the reactants and products by integers so that the number of atoms of each element is the same on both sides of the equation.

For example, the equation for the combustion of carbon is:
\[ \text{C} + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow \text{CO}_2 \]
To balance this equation, we can multiply the coefficient of O₂ by 2 and the coefficient of CO₂ by 2 to get:
\[ \text{C} + 2\text{O}_2 \rightarrow 2\text{CO}_2 \]
Now, the number of atoms of each element is the same on both sides of the equation:
- Carbon: 1 on both sides
- Oxygen: 4 on both sides

The coefficients are the smallest whole numbers that satisfy the equation. If the coefficients are not the smallest whole numbers, the equation is not balanced.

For example, the equation for the combustion of carbon is:
\[ \text{C} + 4\text{O}_2 \rightarrow 4\text{CO}_2 \]
This equation is balanced, but the coefficients are not the smallest whole numbers. The correct balanced equation is:
