The rate of a photochemical reaction catalyzed by a photosensitensorizer is directly proportional to the concentration of the photosensitensorizer. This is because the rate of light absorption by the photosensitensorizer is directly proportional to its concentration. When the photosensitensorizer absorbs light, it becomes excited and can then transfer its energy to the reactants, leading to the photochemical reaction. Therefore, increasing the concentration of the photosensitensorizer will lead to a higher rate of light absorption, which in turn will result in a faster rate of the photochemical reaction.

