   A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 + m_v} = \frac{100}{1 + 0.1} = 100 / 1.1 \approx 90.91 \text{ V/V}
   \]

2. **Determine the new open-loop gain \( A_v' \):**
   The gain decreases by 6 dB, which means the new gain is:
   \[
   A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{10^{0.6}} = \frac{100}{10^{0.6}} \approx 63.1 \text{ V/V}
   \]
   Thus, the new open-loop gain is:
   \[
   A_v' = \frac{100}{10^{0.6}} \approx 63.1 \text{ V/V}
   \]

3. **Calculate the new closed-loop gain \( A_{vf}' \):**
   \[
   A_{vf}' = \frac{A_v'}{1 + m_v} = \frac{63.1}{1 + 0.1} = 63.1 / 1.1 \approx 56.91 \text{ V/V}
   \]

4. **Calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain:**
   \[
   \text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{56.91 - 90.91}{90.91} \right) \times 100 \approx -33.1\%
   \]

