To solve this problem, we need to consider the concept of time dilation in special relativity. Time dilation occurs when an object is moving at a high speed relative to an observer. The time experienced by the moving object (the clock on the ship) will be shorter than the time experienced by the observer at rest (the clock on Earth).

The time dilation formula is given by:

Δt' = Δt / √(1 - v^2/c^2)

where Δt' is the time experienced by the observer on Earth, Δt is the time experienced by the moving object (clock on the ship), v is the relative velocity between the two observers, and c is the speed of light.

In this problem, we are given that the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth. We can assume that the clock in the cargo bay is in the same frame of reference as the clock in the cockpit, so it will experience the same time dilation effect. Therefore, the time experienced by the clock in the cargo bar is also 3 years.

Now, we can use the time dilation formula to find the time experienced by the clock on Earth:

Δt' = 3 years / √(1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2)

Δt' = 3 years / √(1 - 0.36)

Δt' = 3 years / √(0.64)

Δt' = 3 years / 0.8

Δt' = 3.75 years

