One mole of an ideal gas is contained under a weightless piston in a vertical cylinder at temperature \( T \). The space above the piston is open to the atmosphere. What work must be performed to isothermally expand the gas volume under the piston by a factor of \( n \) by slowly raising the piston? Assume negligible friction between the piston and the cylinder walls.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The work done during an isothermal expansion of an ideal gas is given by:  
\[ W = nRT \ln \left( \frac{V_f}{V_i} \right] \]  
where:  
- \( n \) is the number of moles of the gas,  
- \( R \) is the ideal gas constant,  
- \( T \) is the temperature of the gas,  
- \( V_i \) is the initial volume of the gas,  
- \( V_f \) is the final volume of the gas.  

In this problem, the volume is expanded by a factor of \( n \), so \( V_f = n V_i \). Substituting this into the work equation:  
\[ W = nRT \ln \left( \frac{n V_i}{V_i} \right) = nRT \ln(n) \]  

Thus, the work done is:  
\[ \boxed{nRT \ln(n)} \]",relevance,"# Task Description
