   The mass flow rate \( \dot{m} = 0.3 \, \text{kg/s} \) and the density of air at 20°C and 1 atm is approximately \( \rho = 1.196 \, \text{kg/m}^3 \). The velocity at the inlet is:
   \[
   \dot{m} = \rho A_i V_i \implies V_i = \frac{\dot{m}}{\rho A_i}
   \]
   Assuming the flow is uniform at the inlet, the area \( A_i \) is the cross-sectional area of the annular flow at the inlet. The flow is annular, so the area is the difference between the outer and inner areas:
   \[
   A_i = \pi (D_i/2)^2 - \pi (D_i - 2r)^2
   \]
   where \( D_i \) is the diameter of the annular flow at the inlet and \( r \) is the radius of the central conical body.

2. **Velocity at the Outlet**:
   The velocity at the outer wall of the conical body equals the inlet velocity \( V_i \). The velocity at the inner wall is the same as the velocity of the central conical body.

