- \( c \) is the speed of light in a vacuum (\( 3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s} \))
- \( \lambda \) is the wavelength of the radiation
- \( h \) is the Planck's constant (\( 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s} \))
- \( \nu \) is the frequency of the radiation, related to wavelength by \( \nu = \frac{c}{\lambda} \)

This formula represents the spectral density of thermal radiation, which is the power emitted per unit area, unit wavelength. 

