</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the standard free energy change for the hydrolysis of ATP at 37°C, we first need to determine the standard free energy change for the hydrolysis reaction:

\[
\text{ATP} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{ADP} + \text{P}_i
\]

Given the standard free energy change for the synthesis of ATP:

\[
\text{ADP} + \text{P}_i \rightarrow \text{ATP} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \quad \Delta G^\circ = +30.5 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]

The hydrolysis reaction is the reverse of the synthesis reaction. Therefore, the standard free energy change for hydrolysis is the negative of the standard free energy change for synthesis:

\[
\text{ATP} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{ADP} + \text{P}_i \quad \Delta G^\circ = -30.5 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]

However, the standard free energy change is temperature-dependent. At 25°C, the standard free energy change for ATP hydrolysis is approximately -30.5 kJ/mol. To calculate the value at 37°C, we can use the van't Hoff equation:

\[
\ln \left( \frac{\Delta G^\circ_2}{\Delta G^\circ_1} \right) = -\frac{R}{R} \left( \frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2} \right)
\]

Where:
- \( R = 8.314 \text{ J/mol·K} \)
- \( T_1 = 298 \text{ K} \) (25°C)
- \( T_2 = 310 \text{ K} \) (37°C)

Substituting the values:

\[
\ln \left( \frac{\Delta G^\circ_{37}}{-30.5} \right) = -\frac{8.314}{310 - 298}
\]

\[
\ln \left( \frac{\Delta G^\circ_{37}}{-30.5} \right) = -\frac{8.314}{12}
\]

\[
\ln \left( \frac{\Delta G^\circ_{37}}{-30.5} \right) = -0.6878
\]

Taking the natural logarithm:

\[
\frac{\Delta G^\circ_{37}}{-30.5} = e^{-0.6878} \approx 0.516
\]

\[
\Delta G^\circ_{37} = -30.5 \times 0.516 \approx -15.8 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]

**Final Answer:** The standard free energy change for the hydrolysis of ATP at 37°C is \(\boxed{-15.8 \text{ kJ/mol}}\).
